model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521554 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.05x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521560 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521585 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521585 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521585 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521586 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521586 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521586 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "77086ede-11b7-5f95-8032-15b20f19c4f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186370423 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
